  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q28-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "28",
    "prompt": "Let $g(x)$ be an even function and $f(x)$ be any function. Let $h(x)=f(g(x))$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q28-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $h(-x)=f(g(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Since $g$ is even, $g(-x)=g(x)$, so $h(-x)=f(g(-x))=f(g(x))$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(g(-x))\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $g$ is even, $g(-x)=g(x)$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(g(x))\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q28-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why the function $h(x)$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Since $h(-x)=f(g(x))=h(x)$, $h$ is even.",
          "worked": "Part a gives $h(-x)=f(g(x))=h(x)$. Therefore $h$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q28-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Therefore, what can you say about the composition of two functions if the “inside” function is even?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "It is even.",
          "worked": "The composite function is even because an even inside function gives $h(-x)=h(x)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q28-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Similarly, show that if $f(x)$ is even and $g(x)$ is odd, then $h(x)=f(g(x))$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=f(g(-x))=f(-g(x))=f(g(x))=h(x)$, so $h$ is even.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(g(-x))\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $g$ is odd, $g(-x)=-g(x)$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(-g(x))\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $f$ is even, $f(-g(x))=f(g(x))$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(g(x))=h(x)\\end{aligned}$$\nTherefore $h$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q28-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Similarly, show that if $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are both odd, then $h(x)=f(g(x))$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=f(g(-x))=f(-g(x))=-f(g(x))=-h(x)$, so $h$ is odd.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(g(-x))\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $g$ is odd, $g(-x)=-g(x)$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=f(-g(x))\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $f$ is also odd, $f(-g(x))=-f(g(x))$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}h(-x)&=-f(g(x))=-h(x)\\end{aligned}$$\nTherefore $h$ is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      }
    ]
  },
